pdf-appearance: encode CJK annotation text in a collection that has the glyphs (issue #6082)

Free text (and form field) text is written to the appearance stream as UCS-2
codes resolved through one of the Uni*-UCS2-H CMaps. Which collection is used
came from the annotation's language, and with no /Lang that defaulted to
Adobe-Japan1 - which has no CID for the simplified-only Chinese characters, so
they came out blank: "广义定义的组合数" rendered as "广定的合数".

Anything the simple path could not encode fell back to the rich (HTML) layout
path instead, which embeds a whole fallback font - 5 MB of Droid Sans Fallback
for CJK - and draws the text in that face rather than the annotation's own. A
fullwidth comma was enough to trigger it, so ordinary Chinese free text got a
5 MB appearance in a heavier typeface.

- Guess the language from the text itself when there is no /Lang and the DA
  font name says nothing, so Chinese text picks Adobe-GB1 and Japanese text
  (kana) picks Adobe-Japan1.
- Check each character against the collection's CMap and move it to one that
  has it rather than dropping it, so an explicit but wrong /Lang costs a mixed
  face, not missing text.
- A symbol none of the 8-bit encodings has (fullwidth punctuation, ∈, ...) goes
  in the CJK collection the rest of the text uses instead of becoming a bullet.
- Only take the rich layout path when the walk really cannot write a character,
  and decide that with the same walk write_string uses (add_required_fonts is
  rewritten on top of it too, so the two cannot disagree about which fonts the
  stream references).

Also fixes pdf_guess_text_language_from_font_name, whose tests were negated:
"if (!strstr(name, "SimFang"))" is true for every name that is not SimFang, so
the first line matched everything and every annotation came out as zh_Hans.

Fixes https://github.com/sumatrapdfreader/sumatrapdf/issues/6082
